  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM EDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nMain forecast concern for this package is overnight lows and \nsubsequent frost headlines. Surface gradient tightened a bit today \nas low pressure and a weak front crossed northern Ontario. However, \nthe gradient relaxes substantially overnight as front exits and high \npressure rebuilds over the Great Lakes. Calm winds and clear skies \nset the stage for ideal radiational cooling. Most 12Z guidance has \nupper 30s for lows tonight but roughly 25% has mid 30s and there are \na few members (10%) suggesting 33-34F possible. Given ideal \nradiational cooling and recent performance, decided to lean toward \nthe 25th percentile with 35-37F for most locations. Surface \ndewpoints are a little low for a widespread/heavy frost but areas of \nfrost will certainly be possible. Much of our CWA (especially our \nsouthern half) did not experience a widespread, killing freeze the \npast two nights. Therefore, the growing season will continue for IN \nand OH and a frost advisory has been issued for tonight/early Mon. \nColder temps and climatology supported an end to the growing season \nfor the state of Michigan so no headlines will be issued there.\n\nA gradual moderating trend is still expected this week as high \npressure slowly drifts east. Highs on Mon climb into the upper 60s \nand hover around 70F through the weekend. The gradient remains weak \nMonday night but there are indications of some passing high clouds \nand a slightly warmer afternoon should keep overnight lows in the \nupper 30s/near 40F for most. After Monday night, lows remains safely \nin the 40s the rest of the week. Don't anticipate needing another \nfrost advisory at this time. Afternoon RH values near 30 percent are \npossible each day but winds remain light and will minimize fire wx \nconcerns. There is virtually no rain in sight for the next 10 days.\nWeak fronts pass to the north Wed and Sun but strong ridging remains\nlocked over the Ohio Valley.",
